course: dictionary
lesson: 5389
title: Knowledge and the words around it
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Knowledge is a noun, and it is uncountable: you do not have three knowledges, you have some knowledge. It means information, facts and understanding that you have learned.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is meant. «Lessons build knowledge» means knowledge grows as you learn. «He did it without my knowledge» means you were not aware of it, so knowledge here means knowing about something.
explain: Look for phrases that often go with the word. «General knowledge» means facts about many things, «common knowledge» means something everybody knows, and «to my knowledge» means as far as I know. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Knowledge is a noun, and it is uncountable: we say some knowledge, not a knowledge.
fact: Knowledge means information, facts and understanding that you have learned.
fact: «Without someone's knowledge» means without them knowing about it.
fact: «To my knowledge» means as far as I know.
fact: Knowledge that comes from what others tell us is called testimony, and it is wise to trust good sources while still asking, «how do they know?»
exercise: «Lessons build knowledge, so each one adds to what you know.» What does knowledge mean here? | A loud noise | *Information and facts you have learned | Something you can count, like apples | A type of school bag
exercise: «He did it without my knowledge.» What does knowledge mean in this sentence? | A large, heavy book | *Awareness: I did not know about it | Permission that I gave him | Being very clever at maths
exercise: Which phrase means as far as I know? | *to my knowledge | a thirst for knowledge | general knowledge | common knowledge
test: Common knowledge means: | A secret only one person knows | A kind of pen | *Something everybody knows | A new invention
test: Knowledge that comes from what others tell us is called what? | a cymbal | *testimony | a chatbot | a knowledge cutoff
exam: Jayden says knowledge is a countable noun, so we can say three knowledges. Is he right? | Yes, it is countable like apples | No, because it is a verb | Yes, but only in the plural | *No, knowledge is uncountable, so we say some knowledge
